The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<text>
failed:
  usb_put_dev(as102_dev->bus_adap.usb_dev);
  usb_set_intfdata(intf, NULL);
  kfree(as102_dev);
  return ret;
</text>
Reason:
After `usb_register_dev()` succeeds, if an error occurs later (e.g., in `as102_alloc_usb_stream_buffer()` or `as102_dvb_register()`), the code jumps to `failed_dvb` or `failed_stream`, which then falls through to the `failed` label. This label frees `as102_dev` immediately via `kfree()`. However, userspace may have already opened a file descriptor to the device before the error occurred. When that file descriptor is closed later, the `.release()` callback (`as102_release()`) will access or free the already freed `as102_dev`, leading to a use-after-free (UAF) and double-free vulnerability.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
failed_stream:
+	usb_set_intfdata(intf, NULL);
 	usb_deregister_dev(intf, &as102_usb_class_driver);
+	return ret;
</text>
Reason:
When an error occurs after `usb_register_dev()` has succeeded, the fix replaces the fall-through to the `failed` label with a direct return after deregistering the device. By setting the interface data to `NULL` and calling `usb_deregister_dev()`, no new open file descriptors can be created, and the device node is removed. The `return ret` prevents the immediate `kfree(as102_dev)`. Memory deallocation is now deferred to the `.release()` callback (through the `kref` mechanism), ensuring that `as102_dev` is only freed after all open file descriptors have been closed, thus eliminating the race condition.

